<subsection xml:id="subsection-1-4-1">
    <title>The Derivative</title>
    <p>
        The average velocity of an object over a time interval <m>\Delta t</m> is defined as its change in its position <m>\Delta s</m> divided by <m>\Delta t</m>.  
        That is, <m>\displaystyle v_{avg} = \frac{\Delta s}{\Delta t}</m>.  If a function <m>f(x)</m> models movement of an object, 
        then the average rate of change of that object over a time interval <m>\Delta x</m> is given by <m>\displaystyle ARC = \frac{\Delta f}{\Delta x}</m>, 
        where <m>\Delta f</m> is the total change in the function.  
        So, if the time interval is from <m>x=a</m> to <m>x=b</m>, 
        then the average rate of change in <m>f(x)</m> is given by <m>\displaystyle ARC_{[a,b]} = \frac{f(b) - f(a)}{b-a}</m>.  
        Graphically, if we draw a line through the points <m>(a,f(a))</m> and <m>(b,f(b))</m>, 
        then its slope, <m>\displaystyle \frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}</m> is the average rate of change of the function from <m>x=a</m> to <m>x=b</m>.  
        We call such a line a <em>secant line</em>.  
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="ARC-graph">
        <caption>Average rate of change as slope of the secant line</caption>
        <image width="80%" xml:id="secant-line">
            <latex-image>
                \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw[&lt;->,thick] (-5,0)--(5,0);
                    \draw[&lt;->,thick] (0,-5)--(0,5);
                    \draw[domain=-2.1:4.1,smooth,variable=\x,blue,&lt;->, thick] plot ({\x},{0.25*\x*\x*\x -0.75*\x*\x - 0.25*\x + 1});
                    \draw[blue] (3.6,4) node{$f(x)$};
                    \draw (-1.5,0.2)--(-1.5,-0.2);
                    \filldraw (-1.5,-1.15625) circle (2.5pt) node[anchor = east]{$(a,f(a))$};
                    \draw (3.7,-0.2)--(3.7,0.2);
                    \filldraw (3.7,2.47075) circle(2.5pt) node[anchor=south east]{$(b,f(b))$};
                    \draw (3.7,-0.4) node{$b$};
                    \draw (-1.5,0.4) node{$a$};
                    \draw[thick,red] (-4.7,-3.38825)--(4.7,3.16825);
                    \draw (2,-2) node{$ARC_{[a,b]} = $ slope};
                    \draw (3.23,-2.7) node{$\displaystyle = \frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}$};
                    \draw[red,->] (1.2,-1.5)--(-.65,-.7);
                \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
        Average rates of change are fine, but a more interesting concept is the <em>{instantaneous velocity</em> of an object - the velocity of an object at a singular point in time.  
        We can approximate instantaneous velocity by taking very small time intervals.  
        Let <m>a</m>> be the starting time and let $h$ be some time interval (so ending time is <m>a+h</m>).  
        Then we can change our average rate of change function to <m>{\displaystyle ARC_{[a,a+h]} = \frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h}}</m>.  
        When <m>h\approx 0</m>, then the average rate of change is approximately the instantaneous rate of change.  
        Of course, we can't have <m>h=0</m>, but we can make <m>h</m> as small as we would like - the smaller the better.  
        (You smell that?  
        That's the impending arrival of limits.)
    </p>

    <p>
        We can find the instantaneous velocity of a object by taking the limit as <m>h</m> approaches <m>0</m> of our average rate of change function, assuming that limit exists.  
        This limit is called the <term>derivative</term><idx>Derivative</idx> of <m>>f(x)</m> at <m>x=a</m>.  
        It is denoted by <alert><m>f'(a)</m></alert>.
    </p>
    
    <definition xml:id="def-derivative">
        <statement>
            <p>
                The <term>derivative of <m>f(x)</m> at <m>x=a</m></term> is
                <md>f'(a) = \lim_{h\rightarrow 0} \frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h},</md>
                provided that the limit exists.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
        Graphically, the derivative is the slope of the line that locally intersects the function $f(x)$ at only the point <m>(a,f(a))</m>.  
        That is, it is the limit of the secant lines as $b$ approaches <m>a</m>.  
        We call this line the <em>tangent line</em>.  
        This is visualized below.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="derivative-function-graph">
        <caption>Average rate of change as slope of the secant line</caption>
        <image width="80%" xml:id="derivative-graph">
            <latex-image>
                \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw[&lt;->,thick] (-5,0)--(5,0);
                    \draw[&lt;->,thick] (0,-5)--(0,5);
                    \draw[domain=-2.1:4.1,smooth,variable=\x,blue,&lt;->, thick] plot ({\x},{0.25*\x*\x*\x -0.75*\x*\x - 0.25*\x + 1});
                    \draw[blue] (3.6,4) node{$f(x)$};
                    \draw[red] (-4.7,-3.38825)--(4.7,3.16825);
                    \filldraw (-1.5,-1.15625) circle (2.5pt) node[anchor = east]{$(a,f(a))$};
                    \filldraw (3.7,2.47075) circle(2.5pt);
                    \filldraw (2.861,0) circle(2.5pt);
                    \draw[orange] (-4.7,-2)--(4.7,0.4876);
                    \node[rotate=16] at (1,-0.75) {\small{nope}};
                    \node[rotate=36] at (2.4,1.9) {\small{not even close}};
                    \filldraw (0,1) circle (2.5pt);
                    \draw[purple] (-4.104,-4.9)--(2.78,5);
                    \node[rotate=54] at (1.4,3.5) {\small{getting closer}};
                    \filldraw (-0.7,.72175) circle(2.5pt);
                    \draw[green] (-3.052,-4.8)--(1.037,4.8);
                    \node[rotate=65] at (0.3,3.7) {\small{almost there}};
                    \draw[thick,red] (-2.488,-4.8)--(0.11525,4.8);
                    \draw (-2,3) node{\small{Oh, yeah! That's}};
                    \draw (-2.02,2.7) node{\small{the tangent line.}};
                    \draw[red](-.75,3)--(-.5,3);
                \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
        Suppose we would like to find the instantaneous rate of change of a function at multiple points.  
        It would be handy to have a function that could give us these values rather than calculating the derivative at several points.  
        Fortunately, such a function exists and is called the <term>derivative function</term>.<idx>derivative function</idx>
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-derivative-function">
        <statement>
            <p>
                The <term>derivative function of <m>f(x)</m></term>
                is
                <md>{f'(x) = \lim_{h\rightarrow 0} \frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}},</md>
                provided that the limit exits.     
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
        Just for the sake of completion, we will find the derivative function of <m>f(x) = x^3 - 4x</m> using the limit definition.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="ex-derivative-function">
        <title>Derivative of <m>f(x) = x^3 - 4x</m></title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the derivative function of <m>{f(x) = x^3 - 4x}</m>, using the limit definition of a derivative.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <md>
                    \begin{aligned}
                        f'(x) &amp;= \lim_{h\rightarrow 0} \frac{(x+h)^3 - 4(x+h) - (x^3 - 4x)}{h}\\
                        &amp;= \lim_{h\rightarrow 0} \frac{x^3 + 3x^2 h + 3xh^2 + h^3 - 4x - 4h - x^3 + 4x}{h}\\
                        &amp;= \lim_{h\rightarrow 0} \frac{3x^2 h + 3xh^2 + h^3 - 4h}{h}\\
                        &amp;= \lim_{h\rightarrow 0} \frac{h(3x^2 - 4 + 3xh + h^2)}{h}\\
                        &amp;= \lim_{h\rightarrow 0} 3x^2 - 4 + 3xh + h^2\\
                        &amp;= 3x^2 - 4 + 0 + 0\\
                        &amp;= 3x^2 - 4\\
                    \end{aligned}
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <p>
        There.  
        We did an example.  
        Now let's never use the limit definition to calculate a derivative again.  
        The important thing to take away from this section is that the derivative represents the rate of change of a function at a specified point.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="ex-abs-x">
        <title>Derivative of <m>|x|</m></title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Explain why the derivative of <m>f(x) = |x|</m> does not exist at <m>x=0</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Consider secant lines for <m>|x|</m> to the left and right of <m>x=0</m>.
                Take a secant line from <m>x=a</m> to <m>x=0</m> for <m>a&lt; 0</m>.
                The slope of this secant line is <m>{\frac{f(0)-f(a)}{0-a} = \frac{0 - |a|}{-a} = \frac{-(-a)}{-a} = -1}</m>.
                (Note that <m>|a| = -a</m> since <m>a&lt; 0</m>.)
                So, all secant lines to the left of <m>x = 0</m> will have slope <m>-1</m>.
            </p>

            <p>
                Take a secant line from <m>x=0</m> to <m>x=b</m> with <m>b>0</m>.
                The slope of this secant line is <m>{\frac{f(b)-f(0)}{b-0} = \frac{|b| - 0}{b} = \frac{b}{b} = 1}</m>.
                So, all secant lines to the right of <m>x = 0</m> will have slope <m>1</m>.
            </p>

            <p>
                Now consider the full limit of the slope of secant lines at <m>x=0</m>.
                To the left, the limit is simply <m>-1</m>.  
                To the right, the limit is <m>1</m>.
                Since the left and right limits are not equal, the limit does not exist.
                So, the derivative cannot exist at <m>x=0</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>
</subsection>